+# ADR-010: Runtime UI Protocol
+
+**Kind**: Implementation decision
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Deciders**: Flywheel Core Team
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+Every client and the runtime communicate through one typed `RuntimeClient` protocol. `src/protocol/` is the published client contract: the wire types, schemas, patch classification, delivery-policy table, and call-policy table live there, and it is the only `src` surface an out-of-tree client compiles against; the in-tree TUI reaches the runtime through `orchestration/`, as its module boundary requires. The protocol has three equal production transports: the account service socket with an authenticated project-selection preface for the terminal and editor, an authenticated loopback WebSocket for a machine-local browser, and the relay reverse transport for paired devices (ADR-007). Every transport carries the same MsgPack RPC frames to the selected project composition. Initial and recovery state arrives through cursor-anchored snapshots and queries. Live changes arrive as sequenced, cursor-tagged patches.
+
+One runtime sequencer mints patch positions and prior-position links. Each client receiver classifies patches against its held cursor, applies contiguous updates, ignores stale updates, holds out-of-order updates, and requests policy-appropriate recovery when a gap cannot close. Classification is shared code, not a per-client convention: every receiver evaluates `classifyRuntimeUiPatch`. `protocol/client/` is the shared client core for out-of-tree clients: the self-recovering connection over any Effect `Socket` layer with its lanes, the silence deadline, invalidation, and health, the held view for a receiver of one whole view, the sealed socket that wraps any `Socket` in an authenticated end-to-end cipher, the relay channel adapter and device handshake, the cursor-intake receiver, the transcript store with its bounded resident window, the residency that decides which sessions a client keeps resident and when each one reads, the transcript delta application, the session reading family (`session-reading-family.ts`), and the held session detail (`held-session-details.ts`). The session reading family holds one session-scoped domain, such as budget or status, for every session a client shows. It reads each such session once on each lane, even when a live patch already delivered its view, sends the reads asked for in one task as one read, and reads every session it held again when the next lane opens. Both clients read their session domains through it; the terminal adds only a Solid store for the views, and the web client adds only the rule that keeps the patches of sessions it does not show. The held session detail follows the `session_detail` cursor of each session a client shows, keeps that session's whole detail view current through ordered row replacements, and resolves a session's parent route to its chat. The web client and the terminal both use it; the terminal adds only a Solid binding that gives each held session its own signal, so a change to one session wakes only that session's readers. The shared family intake (`protocol/client/family-intake.ts`), under the session reading family and the TUI's in-tree receiver (`tui/hooks/create-ordered-patch-receiver.ts`), implements the same classification contract with its own recovery policy, resnapshotting immediately on a gap and holding a bounded set of patches.
+
+Transcript residency follows the reader's intent. Before the shared store commits or trims a window, the client supplies whether the reader follows the live end and the exact identities of visible blocks. Recovery for a reader away from the live end requests that protected span, including after reconnect, coordinate rebase, or delta divergence. The runtime resolves identities through its exact block index and returns authoritative membership and current coordinates at one output-window snapshot position. It returns every surviving protected block in one contiguous interval. Soft block and byte budgets trim surrounding rows, never the protected span. A page omission is not deletion evidence. An incomplete, unavailable, or failed response leaves the held presentation non-current. Only authoritative absence can retire a protected subject, with the nearest surviving protected or prior neighbour taking its place. A following reader still follows the live end. Explicit latest and search navigation may install their requested destinations. An ordinary missing after-block anchor reports its tail substitution; it does not authorize replacing a protected reading span.
+
+A lane is one attach of a client connection. It opens when the client has written its event subscription to an open socket, sealed when the socket crosses the relay, and it ends when that subscription ends. The connection owns the lane id and `onLane`, which calls a listener at once while a lane is open and again each time a new lane opens. The runtime's first frame on each subscription, `runtime.connected`, carries only its type: the connection consumes it as evidence that the runtime answers and never forwards it to listeners. A lane's end reaches listeners as one global `runtime.invalidate` with reason `reconnect_required`. At that break a receiver voids what the lane covered and reads nothing. It reads its whole view once when the next lane opens, and never at construction, so one lane gives one seed read per receiver. A client that keeps several sessions resident attaches the receivers of each session to the lanes that the shared residency (`protocol/client/residency.ts`) hands that session. The residency is the one listener on the connection's lanes and fans them out to its sessions. At a lane break, every resident session that no surface shows goes dormant, and so does a session whose last surface lets it go before the next lane opens: its receivers hear the break, and then no patch and no lane opening, so they read nothing and the sessions the person sees read first on the next lane. A client holds nothing on the runtime for a dormant session: no runner and no detail, so the next lane activates and reads only what the person sees (ADR-008). A dormant session wakes when a surface shows it. Its receivers first hear the latest `rebase` withheld on each cursor and then the open lane. A transcript receiver resolves its protected identities in the new coordinates before adopting later deltas; a coordinate revision never ends a visible block's lifetime. From a lane break until the next lane's read seeds it, a transcript window proves no live coverage, whether its session is shown or dormant. The web client keeps the question dock mounted with its local disclosure, picks, drafts, and focus. It disables Answer and Skip in place until the window is current, then removes the dock only when current data shows its question answered or cancelled. Resume also waits for a current window. The terminal reads every resident session on each lane, so its residency has no dormancy. Every other invalidation reason, such as `overflow`, `rebase`, or `protocol_violation`, keeps its own read at once for a receiver that is not dormant. A read that fails with its lane shows no failure and does not retry: the attach failed, the lane broke or closed, or the runtime refused the client with `client-mismatch` or `protocol-mismatch`, and the next lane reads it again. A receiver of one whole view, such as auth status, update status, or the module selection, uses the shared held view (`protocol/client/held-view.ts`). The held view reads on each lane, adopts the read before it applies the patches it held while the read ran, and reads again at once on a gap, because every patch carries the whole view and a wait gains nothing. The receivers of a resident session use the lanes of the residency. Detail holds, the catalog, and every other receiver of the whole client use `onLane` directly. On a cold load the web client opens the transcript of the session it was asked for beside the catalog read, not after it. A reloaded page is asked for the session it showed: the client saves the reader's place in the page's session storage whenever the page is hidden or unloads, drops it when the page is shown again, and restores it at the next mount. The local host serves each session at its own path and asks for the session that the page's path names, which takes precedence over the restored place. The client opens that session like a tap, even a child that no catalog lists: the session resolves through `sessionDetail`, and only the runtime's answer that the session is gone opens the default session, with a notice that says so.
+
+The shared held view and family intake own retry admission for their reads through `protocol/client/background-reads.ts`. A failed read that was not lost with its lane retries with backoff while the lane stays open. A lane break and disposal cancel pending retries, and a new lane starts its reads immediately. The last view stays visible until a read or patch replaces it. Readers on one query transport share one failure notice, and each read episode reports its first failure once. A read that times out while the runtime is silent retries without a notice of its own because connection health owns that condition.
+
+The runtime serves a call written after a subscription only once that subscription is registered. `RuntimeEvents` is the first message on each socket that a client's event loop dials. A call that a client makes before its event loop runs can open a socket that a later subscription shares, and the runtime serves that call at once, because it arrived before the subscription. The runtime records a gate for each subscription request at once when the request arrives, on the per-connection record that ends with the disconnect, and opens the gate when the subscription registers, fails, or exits. A second subscription on one socket has its own gate. So a read that a client starts when a lane opens sees every change that the lane does not carry, and needs no position from the connected frame.
+
+While its event loop runs and it is connecting or recovering, a connection holds each call until the next lane instead of failing it, and a best-effort call's time in that wait counts against its deadline. When the lane opens, the connection sends the waiting calls and the lane's own seed reads in one burst, transcript reads first, because the transcript is what the person waits for. A hold that waited for the lane, which is an activation, a held detail read, or a release of either, is dropped, because a hold lasts only as long as the connection that carries it and each holder holds again on the new lane. A connection that is not attaching, and a caller that never listens for lanes, use the direct path. The connection's Effect runtime yields through `setImmediate` where it exists and through a `MessageChannel` task where it does not, as in every browser, because a nested browser timer waits 4 ms or more and would spread one burst over tens of milliseconds. Each lane sends the runtime one `lane_attach` client diagnostic with the durations of its attach stages and the kind of the previous lane's end, and the runtime records it once at INFO as `runtime.client.lane_attached`, with no content.
+
+There is one out-of-tree client, the web client in `webview/`, and it is hosted rather than ported. The VS Code extension mounts it in a webview, the runtime serves it to a machine-local browser, and the phone app's hosted bootstrap loads it, with the phone shell beside it, from the paired machine through the relay at `/client/<runtimeId>/<identity>/phone/<file>`, under the identity the machine's placements announce to the relay, which the relay answers at `/client/<runtimeId>/current` and never adds to device presence. A host provides the required webview payload: a `Socket` layer, a capability object, and a theme. The client branches on those values, never on host identity. The extension host is a transport and a capability provider, not a client: it pumps protocol bytes through an authenticated account-service attachment without decoding them, answers the client's requests to open a file or run an editor command, and paints its status bar from a summary the client publishes. The machine-local host serves the matching build's assets and connects the client to the same runtime through its token-authenticated, exact-origin loopback WebSocket. Every web host is a pass-through for the client the runtime built: the page it mounts states the client bundle's identity, the client offers that identity at every event attach, and the runtime refuses one that is not its own with a `client-mismatch` protocol error.
+
+One client-owned replacement transaction answers that refusal. It prepares the successor while input stays live, waits for accepted attachment preparation and composition, captures the owners, and commits a versioned `InputReloadCheckpoint`. A change during that write requires another capture and commit. Only the final replacement commit holds new input. Preparation, storage, or host failure releases that hold and leaves the live owners and saved bytes intact with a visible failure. The checkpoint transfers the active Composer, inactive resident drafts, the new-chat draft, exact prepared attachment payloads and marker numbers, question interactions by session and tool-use identity, and semantic focus with selection direction and field scroll. It embeds the existing ReadingState, including block disclosure and reading place, rather than defining a second reading format. Browser hosts use IndexedDB scoped by tab, project, and placement; sessionStorage holds only the small tab id and the existing reading checkpoint. The editor uses native webview state through the same storage contract, and the extension never decodes it. Owners adopt before the checkpoint is consumed. Unknown or malformed bytes remain saved and show a failure. The versioned editor host contract prepares a successor bundle without changing HTML or the bridge, then accepts one commit correlated to the mounted page generation and preparation. Ordinary lane recovery does not replace a client.
+
+Every client, the terminal and the editor host's own connection included, states the protocol version it speaks at every event attach, through the one shared connection in `protocol/client/`. The runtime checks a stated bundle identity first, because a page of another bundle reloads whatever protocol it speaks, and then the protocol version. It refuses another version, or none, with a `protocol-mismatch` protocol error that names the release it runs and the protocol version that release speaks, so the client can tell which side is older. The client shows that refusal and keeps attaching, so it attaches once the two versions agree. The event attach is the one message that crosses protocol versions: its fields keep their meaning in every version, and a runtime ignores attach fields it does not know, so a client of any later version reaches the version check and gets the refusal. A terminal offers no bundle identity, and no transport preface checks a client. A terminal follows a protocol change as a page reloads, without a word on screen: when the account service runs the current build and speaks a protocol the terminal cannot, the terminal writes where the person is into one owner-only launch-state file in the lane's private service directory, suspends its renderer, and replaces its own process image with the build that serves, keeping its pid, the shell's foreground job, and the tty. The place is the foreground session, the open tabs in order, every composer draft with its caret and every open question form, the expanded rows by block identity, each session's scroll place as the live end or a block's absolute index and the rows into it, and the open sessions modal, subagent viewer, workflow card, workflow agent view, or theme modal. The file is a versioned contract between two builds, named by the successor's `FLYWHEEL_LAUNCH_STATE` environment variable and parsed strictly; a build that predates the variable ignores it and starts plain, which an unknown argument would not allow. The successor clears the variable and deletes the file once read, and one of a version or shape it does not know, or for another project, starts the terminal plain with the reason in the log. Where a platform cannot replace a process image the successor runs in the terminal's foreground job with its streams and the terminal exits with the successor's status. Only a successor that cannot start is a fault, shown with the ordinary remedy. A terminal that asked for a restart re-executes the same way into its lane's entry once the new account service serves (ADR-014). A service of another build is asked to retire instead (ADR-014). A terminal attached through the relay (PI-REMOTE-016) cannot become the machine's build, which is on another machine, so it settles releases before it attaches: device presence names the release and protocol each placement runs, and the terminal attaches when they equal its own, waits with a message while the machine runs an earlier release, and otherwise installs the machine's release into its own account through the ordinary update path and re-executes into it, handing the successor the chosen project in its environment. A terminal run from source installs no release; it compares only protocols and says so when they differ. A protocol refusal at attach, which only a placement presence did not describe can produce, takes the same path. The terminal's runtime notice says that it installs the release, or why it could not, and a fault's remedy names a reset on the machine, never on this one. A frame the terminal cannot decode is not taken as an earlier runtime: presence names the release, so only a build its release does not name can send one. Such a terminal never restarts, replaces, or retires the machine's service. Provider sign-in and paid-use review, auth profiles, the role browser, the provider model catalog, and usage are sheets inside the client, so every browser host and the editor offer them alike; the extension host keeps a connection of its own only for the auth notification coordinator, which raises editor notifications while the client is hidden, and for workflow launch.
+
+The protocol also owns non-ordering contracts: user mutations are lossless with respect to client-local deadlines, while retryable reads are best-effort; durable transcript history plus the live runtime tail share one absolute coordinate space; the account holds the executor-resolved chat-default projection; and auth, provider-model, and runtime-config views are scoped, secret-free, and carry the identity needed for stale-write rejection. ADR-018 owns config decoding, revision, mutation, application, and settlement semantics; this ADR owns only their typed delivery across the runtime boundary.
+
+---
+
+## Context
+
+A client and the runtime do not share memory: every client sits across a socket from the runtime process. A plain event stream is also insufficient: a dropped, reordered, or unconsumed update can leave a healthy-looking stale pane.
+
+The protocol therefore needs explicit snapshot anchors, cursor continuity, gap classification, recovery policy, and connection state. Transcript delivery additionally needs one coordinate across the durable derived prefix and active in-memory tail; separate offset spaces make recovery depend on restitching and hidden translation state.
+
+---
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. RuntimeClient Is The Only Client Boundary
+
+`session.claim_composer_returns` is a lossless atomic claim of pending interactive input for a parent chat. The request carries locally owned submission IDs; the runtime claims only returns that name one of those IDs and otherwise answers `no_match`. The shared terminal and web submission owner retains the sending window's identities separately from optimistic transcript rows. Queued child output retires those rows, not sender ownership. A committed non-pending user message, recall, or settled return claim retires the identity. The coordinator never claims an identity learned only from a notice. Only the sending window inserts its ordered copies into its parent draft. A closed sending window leaves the words in the immutable display-only parent transcript notice; no other client inserts them. The return stays claimable by its sending window across disconnection and parent turn settlement. An unclaimed cell stays until the session is deleted. The local draft remains client-owned, so a crash or lost reply after the claim can lose the composer convenience but never the person's words. A new event lane retries pending sender claims. Claims wait until input is ready. No reservation, takeover, draft return ID, or additional replay domain exists.
+
+The client:
+
+- dispatches typed mutations and workflow operations;
+- reads initial or recovery snapshots;
+- performs bounded or query-only reads; and
+- subscribes to live patches.
+
+The contract is a package, not a convention. `src/protocol/` owns every mutation, query, patch, and snapshot type, their schemas, `classifyRuntimeUiPatch` with the delivery-policy table, the call-policy table, and the client core under `protocol/client/`. It imports only Bun-free `infra/` modules, never runtime internals, and a build gate walks its import closure to keep it that way, so a Node-hosted editor extension, a browser, and a Cloudflare Worker all compile against it directly. An out-of-tree client compiles against `src/protocol/` and nothing else in `src`; `bun run check:boundaries` holds `webview/`, `vscode/`, and `relay/` to that rule.
+
+The in-process test transport and the three production transports expose the same interface, served by the same protocol handlers over the same RPC schema. The local socket and loopback WebSocket carry MsgPack directly. The relay reverse transport carries the same MsgPack frames inside its sealed channel. A transport changes framing, admission, and lifetime, never call, cursor, or recovery semantics. The loopback WebSocket admits only the runtime's token, and only from a page whose Origin is a loopback address. The relay transport seals every frame below the protocol under a key only the paired ends hold, and the protocol itself carries no credential. A client does not reach around the boundary to runtime stores, queue internals, SQLite, or provider streams.
+
+The Remote module declares the `remote` workspace domain, its `remoteSnapshot` query, its `remote.changed` patch, and its eight lossless mutations in its protocol home. The registry composes their codecs, routes, deadlines, and editor coverage; each mutation declares `editor: "omitted"`. Its relay wire helpers live beneath that same protocol home and remain published contracts for the phone host. The device side of the relay lives there too, as one relay client that every client reaching a runtime through the relay uses: it pairs from a code, watches each paired machine's presence, keeps the machine and project picker with each project open, asleep, starting, or failed to start, wakes an asleep project and attaches once it reports open, asks a machine that announces its machine placement to create a project from a repository and, once the machine has opened it, answers that project and chooses it as starting when no project is chosen or when its host has no project on screen, as the terminal's picker has not, so a project the person has on screen stays and a created project opens through the same start window as an asleep one, and hands the client a `Socket` layer sealed to the chosen machine's key. The creation exchange is sealed JSON on the machine placement's channel, as the pairing exchange is on the pairing channel, so the relay contract carries it without a change. It holds no device storage: its host supplies the device key, the paired machines, and the last selection through the device store contract, and keeps them where its platform keeps them, as the phone's hosted page does in IndexedDB. The domain publishes the runtime's own reachability as one `latest-collapsible` view: the relay it uses, the control channel's connection state, the paired devices, the count of attached remote clients, and the open pairing window with the code a QR renders. Pairing and revocation are lossless mutations on that domain. Disconnecting a stored credential and renaming an account are protocol mutations too (`auth.account.disconnect`, `auth.account.rename`), served by the same account operations the TUI's auth modal runs in process, so a client that is not a terminal manages accounts without a second authentication path. The module's pure `composer.status` selector supplies both the terminal composer border and the web connection line. Its client homes own the Remote surface and the phone Machines surfaces. Turning Remote off removes their commands, settings, labels, surfaces, and transport effects; the kernel loopback browser path remains available.
+
+The workspace `session` domain carries chats only. Its snapshot, counts, and `session.added`, `session.updated`, and `session.removed` patches exclude child sessions. Strict catalog codecs enforce this boundary at the producer, not through client filtering. A bare subagent or workflow-agent session id resolves through passive `sessionDetail`, independent of catalog membership. A workflow id never resolves as a session.
+
+The workflows protocol module registers workflow-scoped `workflow_detail`, `workflow_view`, and `workflow_live` cursors keyed by `workflowId`. Its session-scoped `workflow_runs` domain lists a chat's bound run ids. Detail exposes run configuration, admitted controls, policy usage, display totals, and attribution availability. Attempt views carry real member session ids and historical session ids. Passive workflow holds acquire no session residence and execute no agent. Agent status, transcript, budget, background work, models, and changes use ordinary session domains.
+
+Each client renders workflow agents with its ordinary child-session conversation body. The workflows client module supplies the workflow header, brief disclosure label, and agent navigation. It does not own another transcript reader or status implementation. Workflow cards remain workflow-scoped surfaces. Workflow and session targets stay distinct in messages, controls, saved routes, and launch results.
+
+The per-session `session_detail` cursor carries full detail-row replacements and null removal. Only the account's project control client publishes it; an executor refuses a detail snapshot. A client connection holds a session's detail in two ways, and each ends only by its own release: a `session.activate` holds it while the connection shows the session, until a `session.release`, and a detail snapshot holds it while the connection reads it, until a `session_detail.release`. So a client that lets go of a detail it read keeps the runner of a tab it shows. The end of the connection ends both. A client sends `session_detail.release` only after the snapshot that took the hold has answered, so a snapshot that waited behind a client's limit on snapshot reads never takes a hold after its release. The publisher reads only sessions that some connection holds, and forgets a session when its last hold ends. It reads a held session again only when an input of its row can have changed: its metadata, an edge of what an Interrupt can cut, an edge of its provider connection, which its notices state, its workflow progress, a fact a module's admission names, a conversation batch that records how a turn ended, the parent tool result that settles a child, or the first totals of a row that the last read found loading, which the summary index or the executor's budget reports. A loading row waits for its totals only once. A row that stays loading after that wait, such as one whose budget summary failed, is read again at the backoff of a failed read, and the executor's first totals for it read it at once. Later totals reach clients through the budget domain. Ordinary conversation appends, and activity changes inside a turn, read nothing. Background reads run one at a time on the lane that snapshots share, and each queues the next, so a snapshot waits behind at most one of them. A read publishes its row only when it differs from the row the holders were last given, and a snapshot follows the same rule, so a snapshot that finds a newer row tells the earlier holders, and its own holder drops that patch by position. A failed read keeps its change and is tried again after a backoff, even when nothing else changes, and no other change reads it sooner; one diagnostic records a run of failures and the read that recovers. The catalog row of a held chat carries the admitted actions and stop evidence its detail read found, so a client shows a listed chat from its catalog row and holds detail only for a child or a session the catalog no longer lists. A client that needs one row once, and does not follow it, reads `sessionDetail`, which holds nothing. Row statistics are the values of the last read; detail patches never reseed status, budget, workflow, or output domains.
+
+Clients retain a detail row before its snapshot, ignore unheld patches before cursor intake, and cancel pending reads and release cursors at the last hold. Reconnect snapshots only held roots and child chains. Late snapshots cannot overwrite newer patches or restore released rows. The web client keeps the root chat served while selecting a child; Back follows live parents. The terminal restores a saved child leaf through that same ancestry without creating child tabs or resuming work. No per-parent child-list domain or polling is required.
+
+Directory identity travels the same way. The workspace session snapshot carries the `projectRoot` the runtime opened, and every session catalog row and session detail carries the resolved `executionCwd` selected by the runtime (ADR-013). A client displays both scalar values from the protocol and never resolves a directory of its own. Loadable sessions are ordinary rows with no execution-directory provenance, missing marker, notice, refusal, or directory-repair mutation.
+
+The web shell uses the held project identity to mount its session navigation and selected workspace. Provider readiness and remote placement reports update those surfaces without removing them. A runtime or provider stage fills the pane only before the first project snapshot; for a known project it is advisory and leaves the resident conversation interactive. Dependent actions retain their existing admission checks. The Status Runtime row remains one button, with current health controlling `aria-disabled` and guarded activation. In the terminal, automatic no-provider Auth presentation waits for the existing overlay slot to become free, rechecks provider setup, and opens once per shell. A modal replacement publishes its occupancy atomically, so it offers no vacant slot to automatic dialogs. Explicit Auth navigation opens a fresh editor.
+
+Session catalog, detail, transcript, and startup operations are passive reads. Opening a chat tab attaches its runner idle, and an executor generation change reattaches every tab still open in a client, without resuming its interrupted turn. Every client presents an opened session as idle, with any interrupted work marked in its transcript. Passive reads append no facts. Catalog, detail, and startup reads acquire no worker; a transcript read asks for an executor generation as an open tab does, because only the executor builds a transcript (ADR-007). An explicit user message, Resume (`chat.resume`, `workflow.resume`, `subagent.resume`), or answer to a pending question can continue execution and, when needed, atomically publish recovery evidence with sequence guards before execution begins.
+
+The workspace snapshot carries the account-held chat-default projection. The account seeds each executor generation with that retained state in the private lifecycle start command, then the first public client subscription starts the executor's owned deferred resolution. The account's mandatory private output subscription does not start model work. The projection is `pending`, `none`, or `model` with an exact model and effort; tier vocabulary never crosses this boundary. The account retains the complete state across executor generations and replaces it atomically. Private output-gap recovery adopts the executor snapshot before public invalidation, so a dropped replacement converges through the ordinary resnapshot path. The `chat_default` domain is `latest-collapsible`, so each patch carries the complete current projection and a newer patch supersedes any intermediate value. A cold client can be interactive while the state is `pending`; `none` is a settled no-assignment result. This view does not capture the profile or provider admission that explicit send or resume work captures at click time.
+
+The terminal owns unconfirmed submissions until adopted runtime output carries their client submission identities or successful runtime recall explicitly withdraws them. It displays the input immediately as queued, outside the runtime transcript coordinates. RPC submission success alone does not remove the local row. Each submitted input carries one opaque identity; merged queued inputs retain all constituent identities. The committed human conversation message retains these identities so derived history and recovery can replace local rows without text matching or duplicate output. A message with one identity is also recorded under it: its conversation entry, or the held cell that waits for the next save point, has the key `person:<identity>`, and a workflow's request addendum keeps that key after its commit retires the cell. Every path that records a person's message reads that key before any refusal or side effect, and a `session.send` whose key the session already holds answers `ok` with the identity in `confirmedSubmissionIds` and records nothing. A chat or a child also sets `inTranscript` on its receipt: its transcript shows the message, now or when its reader takes the held cell, so the client keeps the entry queued until that transcript retires it. A workflow has no transcript, so its receipt retires the entry at once. A copy to an interrupted child whose cell still waits starts the child again, as the first copy does. So a client sends a message again under its identity, by itself and in send order, when the lane it was sent on ended before the runtime answered, and the session records it once. Such a message, like one whose send has not been answered yet, stays queued and cannot be recalled until a lane answers it. The client keeps it through a terminal restart, a self-update, and any end of a web page: a page keeps its waiting messages in its input checkpoint while it lives, because a reload or a closed tab runs no code it can rely on. A client can name the chat it opens: a `chat.open` with no initial message can carry a `sessionId` of the session id shape, and the runtime opens that chat once and answers a later copy with the chat that exists, because the chat is the record of the open. The web client names every chat it opens (`protocol/client/chat-open.ts`), so an open lost with its lane goes out again under the same id when a newer lane opens, and a message sent while no chat is open waits for that open and then goes out as any other. The terminal sends such a message on `chat.open` itself, which names no chat, so a second copy would open a second chat: when that creation is lost the message stays failed and editable, and the chat the lost answer named may exist. The identities never enter provider message bodies. A message the runtime refused remains visible and can be recalled for editing and explicit resubmission with a new identity.
+
+Usage is the `subscription-usage` module: its three parameterized reads and three lossless mutations are declared in its protocol home and routed by the module registry. It declares no live domain or cursor. Its queries use the control-read deadline. Auth status and unified Usage operations are explicitly scoped to defaults or one session. Profile-management mutations carry `defaults`, `session`, or `defaults-and-session` scope, so the runtime applies a default change and an optional session mirror as one declared protocol operation. The runtime resolves each scope from account profile state plus session metadata. Usage clients never send a profile id: `usage.open`, `usage.accounts`, `usage.spend`, `usage.history`, `usage.refresh`, and `usage.apply_reset` all carry `RuntimeAuthStatusScope`. The reset mutation is lossless and targets an opaque account source plus the reviewed authority incarnation and revision, so stale review cannot consume another entitlement. The profile Usage authority selects the exact eligible credit and creates or resumes its durable in-flight checkpoint before a provider-registered consumer performs network I/O. The client UUID identifies the logical attempt, while the authority owns the resumed provider idempotency key. Usage views expose the available count and an array of sanitized credits in redemption order, earliest expiry first. Each credit carries only optional title, description, and expiry; clients derive the first credit for review. Provider credit identifiers, credentials, subjects, and credential instance IDs never cross this boundary. `usage.accounts` reads current provider snapshots and source-local quota accounting for the selected exact provider window, with explicit window-unavailable and ledger-unavailable states. `usage.spend` reads an explicit half-open UTC range from the immutable profile request ledger. `usage.history` reads its own requested range from provider observations. Refresh changes provider observations witho