+### 2. One Sequencer Owns Patch Positions
+
+Every live patch carries:
+
+- a domain and session/workspace cursor identity;
+- a sequencer-minted `position`; and
+- the `prevPosition` from which that cursor advances.
+
+Publishers supply payloads, never ordering metadata. Only publication mints a position from the global monotonic source and advances the named cursor's chain. Its `prevPosition` is that cursor's last published position, or zero for its first patch. No read resets or advances a publish chain.
+
+Snapshot positions are observational reads. A concrete cursor snapshot returns its last published position or zero without allocating cursor state. A workspace snapshot returns the existing global published watermark in constant time, including publications in unrelated domains. It neither mints a position nor scans cursor positions. The accompanying data determines coverage: a whole-family snapshot covers that family through its watermark; a partial workflow batch covers only returned requested IDs, including entries with null views. No workspace watermark replaces concrete `outputWindow` recovery: transcript coverage comes only from the transcript read.
+
+The account publication boundary owns status settlement coverage. It retains only the latest private/public output publication pair per resident session and releases those pairs at session removal, executor generation close or replacement, the completion of a private output-gap repair, and facade disposal. A private predecessor missing from a retained pair becomes a public output invalidation, not a falsely contiguous delta; a private invalidation keeps its own reason, because a rebase moves block coordinates and an overflow does not. Generation change and gap repair release every pair at the moment every client is told to re-read, so a session without a pair after that starts a new pair at its next publication instead of costing each client a second read. Before the first such release the account may have attached after publications it never saw, so an uncovered session's first publication with a predecessor becomes an invalidation. Status patches and status queries use the same translation: a covered barrier names a real public output publication; an uncovered barrier carries an opaque recovery requirement, never a private position or null. The client satisfies that requirement only by adopting its own authoritative tail read begun after observing it. An already-running read, workspace watermark, cursor intake, or another client's snapshot receipt is not proof. Recovery uses the existing bounded tail read, merge, and failure paths and retains one requested and one adopted requirement per transcript owner. Reads mint no public positions, and executor replacement never resets the account sequencer.
+
+Each session has one ordered output publish loop. A pass drains every output change committed before it began, and a change committed during a pass waits for the next pass. An idle status waits only for the pass that drains its own session's committed output, so its settled output position names that publication; it never waits for passes that later changes start, such as the card refreshes of a child that streams on. A live tool body larger than the inline limit leaves its row with only a line count, and a client reads the body through `outputBlockBody`, as it reads an omitted historical body. Long streaming text changes split into bounded appends that a client applies in order.
+
+The runtime status projection owns the displayed activity's start time and sends it beside the activity in every status view. It derives this time from the session store's activity start and the reply wait's stored opening time. When a reply wait closes and other background work still parks the session, the projection retains that boundary's runtime time, so the next Waiting status starts there. The projection lives once per runtime and shares these times across snapshots and clients; output updates and client attachment do not reset them. Synthetic waiting during planned replacement has no start time unless a stored reply wait supplies one. The web activity row counts from this runtime time, never from the turn's start or the client's observation. Running transcript rows keep their own clocks, and the finished-turn line keeps the turn total.
+
+### 3. Delivery Policy Is Declared Per Domain
+
+Every UI domain declares exactly one policy:
+
+- `durable-resnapshot`: recover a gap through an authoritative bounded read;
+- `latest-collapsible`: replace local state with the latest complete projection because intermediate patches are not semantically required; or
+- `query-only`: no live stream exists and every read is a fresh query.
+
+Every domain also declares its patch content. A `whole-value` patch states the cursor's whole value, so applying it twice equals applying it once; a sparse total merge qualifies because it is idempotent. A `change` patch states an edit, a fold step, or a signal with no value. A `latest-collapsible` domain always states whole values, and the type of the delivery row admits no other pairing. `output`, `changes`, `background`, `project_notifications`, and `runtime` state changes. `roles` also states changes, because the web client reads each roles patch as a signal that the session's metadata changed and reads its model assignments again. Every other domain states whole values.
+
+Each session's `roles` reading contains its `assignments` map and its runtime-owned `serving` seat. That seat states the selection for its next request and the model picker's session-local role target. The runtime uses the runner's resolution rules; clients never infer the serving seat from role precedence. Session edits publish on that session's existing cursor. Profile admission changes refresh observed readings, and workflow settings changes refresh the affected members. This reading is transient and adds no durable model-binding fact.
+
+The account's public bus publishes on change. It does not publish a whole-value patch that equals the last patch published on its cursor when no read of that cursor's domain came after that publication. The declined patch mints no position and leaves the cursor's chain where it was, so the cursor's next publication names the last position every receiver holds as its predecessor and no receiver sees a gap. A change patch and an invalidation always publish, and an invalidation ends its cursor's baseline. A read can answer a value the bus never published, such as an auth view computed live or a budget a read refreshed, so each read voids the baselines of exactly the domains its answer seeds, in the same synchronous call that takes its position. A cursor read voids its cursor. A workspace read names its domains in `RUNTIME_READ_DOMAINS`, which states for each query the domains it seeds: the workspace snapshot seeds `session`, `chat_default`, `executor`, and `service`; each one-domain snapshot seeds its own domain; `outputWindow` seeds `output`; `sessionChangeSummary` seeds `changes`; `sessionBackgroundWork` seeds `background`; a module snapshot seeds its domain; and every other query seeds none. A completeness gate makes a new query state its row. A client can also seed a catalog row from an answer that has no position: a mutation answer that the account forwards from the executor carries the executor's row, not the account's merged row, and a client's `sessionDetail` read carries the row it found. Each such answer voids that row's cursor when the account returns it. The account's own detail reads void nothing, because no client holds what they read. The executor's bus publishes every patch: its one reader, the account, merges executor patches with its own state and answers some reads from history, so that reader does not always hold the last publication.
+
+The kernel declares its domains in the protocol core. A module declares its domains once, in its protocol home under `src/protocol/modules/<id>.ts`, against the generic contract in `src/protocol/protocol-module.ts`, and the protocol registry `src/protocol/modules.ts` composes every domain table from those declarations: the session and workspace cursor literals, the delivery-policy lookup, the query and patch payload unions, the result codec by discriminant, the client deadline tiers, the client intake bindings, and the editor's coverage table. A module domain is complete when its protocol module declares its scope, its delivery policy, its patch content, its snapshot query with the result codec and deadline tier, its patch codec, and the fold that reads its view from a snapshot and folds a patch into the view the client holds, so a fact-shaped patch and a whole-view patch travel the same intake; a session-scoped domain served from durable history also declares its background codec. A module also declares, in the same home, its queries that are not snapshots, each with a discriminant, its input fields, its result codec, its deadline tier, and an optional editor omission, and its mutations, each with a type literal, its fields, its result codec, its call policy of lossless or retryable, and an optional editor omission. The registry composes the reads of every module, snapshot and query alike, and the mutations of every module into the query and mutation unions, the result codec tables, the deadline and call-policy tables, and the editor coverage rows exactly as it composes domains, and the app service routes a module read to the server and a module mutation to the handler the module's protocol runtime registered under that discriminant, with no module case in the runtime. No table names a module domain, query, or mutation by hand, and the registry's value-import closure stays pure schema so every client can hold it.
+
+The domain union, delivery-policy table, and client intake bindings are compile-time coupled. A new live kernel domain is incomplete until it declares recovery behavior and binds a receiver; a module domain's client binding derives from its registry entry, so the terminal and the web client each bind every module domain through one generic intake per scope and no module names a receiver. This turns a missing pane intake from a runtime stale-state bug into a build failure.
+
+A module's client presence is the slot map, and it is the only way a module reaches a client. `intro.rows` and `status.rows` are declarative rows a pure selector in the module's protocol home computes from that module's own views through a `DomainReader`, so the terminal's welcome card and status modal and the web client's intro card and status sheet render one description: a label, a value, an optional hint, an optional warning tone, and an optional action that runs a runtime command by its catalog id or opens a client surface by its id. A module without a live domain can provide an on-demand status query; clients invoke it when Status opens and render the result through the module's pure protocol selector. A stateful terminal surface can provide a lazy controller that owns its multi-view keyboard and modal lifecycle. `session.strip` is a component per client, rendered above the composer for the foreground session in registry order. `surface.<id>` is a component per client that a runtime `open` outcome or a strip opens by id: a modal in the terminal, which the host lends a reader over the session it opened for, the runtime client for the module's own mutations, and kernel settings plus registered surface navigation; on the web a sheet the app mounts beside its own, or a focus inside the module's strip. The terminal also runs a module's optional presence hook once under the shell's owner, with the foreground reader and the toast sink, for presentation that is neither a strip nor a surface. `toolbar.badges` is a selector per module in its web home: a count, the noun it counts, an optional tone, and one action that opens a surface or runs a command, computed from the module's own views through the same reader, which the web toolbar renders as a chip after the kernel's own controls in registry order; the terminal has no toolbar, and the module's rows carry the reading there. Each client carries one slot host and one module registry, `src/tui/modules.ts` over `src/tui/modules/<id>/` and `webview/src/modules.ts` over `webview/src/modules/<id>/`, and the host names no module: it reads every registered module's rows through one reader over its intake, renders every strip and every badge, runs every presence hook, and resolves every surface id from the registry beside the client's own sheets. A render failure in one module's strip, surface, badge, or presence hook is contained to that slot and reported as one toast naming the module, so a broken module never takes the composer with it.
+
+A query result crosses the wire as an opaque value. The runtime encodes it and the client decodes it with the codec the query's discriminant selects from one table of kernel rows plus the registry, never by a structural first match over a union of result shapes. Broad protocol unions are schemas over their direct leaf members, including leaves nested behind another union, so decoding does not select an empty or partial structural branch before it reaches the discriminant. The background Worker's module snapshot is one generic RPC that carries the domain and the session id; each end selects that domain's background codec the same way.
+
+An unknown module selection is not an opt-out. Every client admits a module's surfaces and contributions until a published selection excludes it. A runtime-published command can therefore open its surface while selection or surface data is still loading. A known exclusion removes the module's contributions and closes its open surfaces. Host-owned acquisition and bug-report surfaces remain available before the first connection.
+
+### 4. The Client Owns Ordered Intake
+
+Each cursor-scoped receiver tracks a covered prefix as its held position, not necessarily the last publication on that cursor.
+
+- A cursor identity mismatch is a gap.
+- A matching patch at or below the held position is stale and ignored.
+- A newer matching patch is a gap only when its `prevPosition` exceeds the held position. Otherwise it applies.
+- When missing patches arrive or resync completes, held patches replay in position order.
+- If the gap cannot close within the receiver's bounds, the domain policy chooses resnapshot, latest replacement, or a fresh query.
+
+For example, publications A1 and B2 followed by a snapshot of A at watermark 2 allow A3 with predecessor 1 to apply. If A3 is lost, A5 with predecessor 3 remains a gap at held position 2. An empty domain can have a nonzero global watermark, and its first later patch with predecessor zero still applies.
+
+Only adoption of a whole-family snapshot clears held cursor entries and replaces the family baseline. Concrete snapshots and partial batches update only their named cursor entries. The web session catalog serializes recovery reads and unseeds the old intake generation before awaiting each snapshot while preserving visible rows. It prunes old intakes before the query, buffers live patches through ordinary cursor intake during the read, installs the response and watermark, and seeds every current intake, including IDs absent from captured rows. Snapshot membership never discards an intake created during that read.
+
+Overflow, reconnection, store divergence, and freshness failure enter this same recovery path. They do not create alternate patch-application logic.
+
+When the runtime bus drops a patch at its pending-delivery limit, every live domain, including `latest-collapsible`, receives a cursor invalidation on the same ordered lane after the queued backlog. Query-only domains have no live recovery obligation. A queued overflow marker coalesces drops for its cursor only until its delivery begins, before any listener callback can snapshot and publish again. A drop after that point earns a successor marker; completion of the earlier marker cannot retire the successor. Recovery reads the domain's authoritative snapshot, not the last published payload, which a domain may define as one row rather than the whole view. A final dropped update therefore recovers without a later data patch.
+
+Client bindings first match their owned cursor and then admit either its invalidation or its normal domain payload. The common receiver consumes raw invalidations before payload refinement; invalidations never reach cache reducers or wait for a missing predecessor. A marker at or below the held snapshot or patch position is already covered and is ignored. A newer marker requests the cursor's authoritative recovery read.
+
+Classification ships with the protocol: every receiver delegates to the same `classifyRuntimeUiPatch` the runtime uses, so both ends evaluate one definition of "in order". `protocol/client/cursor-intake.ts` is the intake implementation for out-of-tree clients, which supply only their apply and recover callbacks. The TUI reads `session_detail` through that same intake, inside the shared held session detail. The shared family intake serves the session reading family of both clients and the TUI's other in-tree receivers, and implements the same classification contract with its own recovery policy: it resnapshots immediately when a gap opens and retains at most 64 distinct held patches and 1 MiB of estimated plain protocol data across its recovery scopes. It deduplicates held deliveries by runtime position and replays the bounded batch in position order after snapshot adoption. On overflow it releases that scope's held data and drops further data patches until the current read settles. It adopts the completed snapshot, then requests one fresh snapshot to cover the dropped interval. Snapshot cancellation, replacement, reconnect, and disposal release retained data and settle waiting callers. Wire shapes are unchanged. Clients compiled with strict predecessor equality must update their compiled protocol contract; changing the runtime alone does not repair those clients.
+
+### 5. Transcript Output Has One Absolute Sequence
+
+Historical output is derived from ADR-008's authoritative conversation and workflow facts. Active output blocks are the uncommitted runtime tail of that same logical transcript, not a second history.
+
+One read path serves windows spanning the durable prefix and live tail. Each response names the snapshot it was answered from with a consistency token; the client merges responses structurally, by absolute index and anchor identity, and records the latest response's token as provenance. Runtime deltas are minted in absolute coordinates at their write boundary. There is no client-visible offset translation, independently indexed live history, or full-snapshot restitching step.
+
+A workflow id is not an output target. Clients select a member's real session id and use ordinary `outputWindow`, `outputRange`, and session cursors for its durable prefix and live tail. The closed initial brief carries the assembled item and inherited input in that conversation. Workflow-scoped `workflow_live` exposes operations, interruption evidence, and current member identity, not another transcript tail. A parent card holds the run's workflow domains and folds its operational tree; an opened agent additionally holds that member's ordinary session domains.
+
+Live and durable message rows share the canonical conversation entry identity reserved before public output. Text runs retain their canonical part indices, and tool rows retain their tool-use identities. Recovery resolves exact block identities, including a later text run within one entry, rather than matching content, timestamps, or old absolute indices.
+
+Gap recovery feeds an authoritative reader-anchored window through ordinary ordered intake. Membership is resolved before body projection. The protected interval extends the minimum-record exception through its final surviving protected block, even when that exceeds the ordinary page budget. The resident bound also protects that finite interval: at the cap it sheds unseen newer rows and detaches instead of removing a visible oldest row. Detached windows still apply identity-targeted updates to retained rows; coordinate-changing deltas recover through the same anchored path. No intervening gap to the live end is fetched. Opaque settlement proof can require a separate fresh tail read, but that proof never installs a tail over a protected window. Anchored-window currency and tail settlement proof remain separate. The live tail is a serving tier, not a replay authority. No durable rendered-output cache, second resident tail, or in-memory replay ring is introduced.
+
+Every output change ships as the smallest true change from the copy the session's clients last received. The write site names what changed: the block it inserted or replaced, the text it appended to a text or thinking block with the content offset, the tools whose live streaming text changed, or a resync of the live tail after a structural rewrite. One delta carries a block's last copy from its drain, never the intermediate ones, and adjacent appends to one block that continue each other ship as one append. The output cursor's publisher, `app-service/shipped-output.ts`, owns the shipped copies: the wire copy of each block by absolute index, the last visible message count, and the live streaming entry of each tool. Every publisher on the output cursor ships through that one owner, which measures each change against its copies, records the new copies, and publishes in one synchronous step, so the copies follow the cursor's order. A block upsert equal to its copy is not sent. A block the clients hold that changed ships as an `edit` that names the block's identity and the edits from the copy to the current block, but only when every read of both copies gives the block itself, with settled thought shown and with it omitted, so that a client that installed the block from an `outputWindow` read holds the same base. A block ships whole when it is new, when another identity held its index, when its change is a rewrite of more than 64 edits, when a read would reshape it (an inline tool body, a legacy diff, an invocation body, a preview that omits lines, settled thought), and always when it is a subagent card, which clients fold by child. A full resync keeps the longest prefix that matches the copies by identity at the same absolute index and changes only that prefix's blocks in place; it truncates at the first index with no matching copy, or at the new end when the tail got shorter, and states the count alone when only the count changed. An `outputWindow` or `outputRange` read serves the session's store, which can hold changes no delta has shipped yet, so the owner serves each read. While a read is in flight, every change ships whole and a resync repaves the tail from its start, so the deltas after the read's position replace whatever it holds. When the read ends, the owner drops each copy that the read served differently and counts the read's blocks as held, so the next change there ships whole and the next resync truncates a block that the read handed out and the session then retracted. Live streaming tool text (model-side input, an execution-output tail, a proposed diff, preview advice) ships as keyed changes per toolUseId: the entry whole when it first appears or changes kind, its removal, or the edits that turn the version last shipped into the current one. A delta never carries the whole streaming map; a resync measures every tool shipped or live, so the tools of a replaced runner end there. A string that grows is an append, a bounded tail that slides is a window that drops a prefix and appends a suffix, an array that grows is a push, and anything else is an assignment at the deepest changed path. An append, a window, and a push name the base they apply to, so a redelivery is idempotent and one against another base is a divergence; an assignment and a deletion carry no base, which is why an edit ships only for a block whose read and delta copies agree. A subagent card's children are the compact rows both clients render there, without the bodies its own session keeps. Nothing on the wire scales with the accumulated state of a stream per change, and the `parallel-sessions` release scenario bounds output bytes per model request and per patch. A read that omits thought content omits it only from settled thinking blocks; a thought still streaming is read whole, because its appends continue from the offset it has reached.
+
+Delta application is likewise shared: `protocol/client/transcript-delta.ts` applies one ordered output delta to a resident view and reports divergence so the caller recovers from the bounded authority instead of guessing. An append whose offset does not match the resident text, or that names text the client does not hold, is a divergence like any other, and so is a streaming edit whose base the client does not hold or whose result the entry schema rejects. A block edit finds its block by identity, because a read can run ahead of the cursor and place the block past its published index; an edit to a block before the resident window is history outside it, and an edit to a block the window lacks, against another base, or whose result the block schema rejects is a divergence. Every client and its tests fold transcript deltas through that one implementation.
+
+User-message rows carry an image count, not image bytes. The existing body read supplies image bytes from the live row or canonical conversation content. A user-message identity that retired before the body read returns an explicit missing result; the shared client refreshes its bounded transcript window and never retains that result as a body. Optional body reads run independently of window replacement and coalesce for the same owner.
+
+The transcript model is folded once as well: `protocol/client/transcript-model.ts` turns a window of blocks into the rows every viewer shows, the stretches of tool work between what the agent said or was told, the blocks no client renders, the queued messages, and the divider before each row. A client owns only its presentation of those rows: the terminal splits a stretch into Explored, Ran, and Executed runs and pins queued messages under the timeline; the web shows one counted heading per stretch and keeps queued messages in place. Which calls fold into one read row is the shared read-run rule. A rule about what the transcript contains or where a divider falls lives in the fold, never in a client.
+
+### 6. Runtime Calls Declare Delivery Semantics
+
+Every unary runtime call is classified in one policy table:
+
+- `Lossless` for user actions and mutations. These calls have no client-local deadline that could report failure after delivery or discard an action before delivery. Runtime failure routing and the process watchdog own liveness.
+- `BestEffort` for retryable reads. These calls have a named bounded deadline and may be retried without duplicating a mutation.
+
+The concrete deadline values are operational tuning in code, not architecture. Adding a call without choosing a delivery class is a protocol defect.
+
+Skill invocation is one of those user mutations. The mutation carries the package id and the user's raw argument text; the runtime resolves the canonical package context from the registry it owns and commits it as the session's next chat turn. No package body crosses the protocol from the client. The turn then renders through the ordinary transcript path, and the model's own `skill` calls render as ordinary tool blocks on that same path, so neither invocation route has a private display channel.
+
+Protocol version 58 exposes no workflow launch mutation. A person asks a chat, whose agent launches a workflow file through its tool. The command catalog publishes no workflow or assignable brief. Workflow Resume, Restart, and Interrupt remain protocol actions. Recorded named runs remain viewable, but Resume, Restart, and messages refuse them and name the retired workflow.
+
+A person's message to any session is one lossless mutation, `session.send`, whatever the kind of the session. The client names only the session. The runtime reads the session's kind and routes the message to its owner: a chat to its actor, a workflow to the workflow host, and a subagent to the module that owns the subagent kind. A workflow and a subagent deliver it through the kernel delivery operation `deliverSessionInput`, and a chat takes it through the ordered send section of its actor. Each owner reads the message's identity against the session's records first, so a copy that a client sends again after a lost answer converges on the one recorded message. A message to an interrupted subagent or workflow resumes it, and a message to an interrupted chat starts a fresh turn. A child row's input mode is `message`, and the row admits `session.send` while the child runs or waits to resume. A session status carries `replyWaits`, the sessions that the session waits on for a reply, each with its id and name. One label function in `src/protocol/client/session-controls.ts` turns a parked status into "Waiting on parent" or "Waiting on <name>", and both clients show that label in the status of the session and on its card in the parent transcript.
+
+Built-in commands cross the same way. The command catalog publishes each built-in as a descriptor, kernel and module alike, under a publication revision that changes on every catalog build. A client renders descriptors and invokes one through the lossless `command.invoke` mutation, carrying the descriptor id, the argument text, the revision it rendered, and the chat session in front when it has one. The runtime executes the handler in the composition that published the descriptor and answers one closed outcome: `none`, `message`, `warning`, `open` with a surface id, `focus-session` with a session id, `stale`, or `unavailable`. A client presents each outcome through its own affordance and never executes a module handler, special-cases a command id, or imports a module composition; a stale revision is refused before execution, so a client can only run what the runtime currently publishes. A command whose whole effect is opening one client surface has no handler: its descriptor names that surface in `opens`, and a client opens it without a round trip. Such a surface therefore opens while the runtime does not answer, and holds the actions that need the runtime (PI-INTERACTION-062). The runtime answers an invocation of such a command with `open` and that surface, for a client that does not read `opens`.
+
+Provider login, disconnect, and rename are lossless mutations, and every client, the terminal included, sends them: the runtime that serves the session owns every credential write, and a client changes only the auth of the runtime it is attached to, never that of the machine it runs on. Each request names the auth profile the client shows; a request that names none writes the runtime's active profile. `auth.login` answers immediately with what the client must do next: an API-key provider answers that it needs a secret, a flow that asks a question before it starts answers with that question and an opaque operation ID, a device-code provider answers with a verification URL, an opaque operation ID, and a code only when the user must enter it while the flow keeps working, and a browser provider answers with the authorization URL and an opaque operation ID. The client answers the question through `auth.login.answer` with that operation ID, and the runtime answers that mutation with the flow's next step. The client host opens a reported URL, unless the client states in `auth.login` that the person is at the runtime's machine, as a terminal attached on that machine does; the runtime then opens each sign-in page there, so a flow whose program takes its own callback completes without a pasted code. That statement is a client hint, and the runtime does not check it. The risk is small, because the runtime makes each URL it opens and a client cannot choose one. A terminal attached through the relay states nothing: it opens the reported page in the person's own browser, shows its address, and takes the code or the address the page ends on. When a browser cannot deliver the machine-local callback, the client submits the callback URL or code through `auth.login.complete` with that operation ID; the runtime rejects stale operations and keeps PKCE state, token exchange, and credential storage on the machine. Secret-bearing login inputs are redacted immediately after protocol decode. While a continuing OAuth flow runs, the `auth` cursor carries the provider's progress messages with that exact operation ID, and the flow ends there exactly once, with that operation ID, the provider ID, and either success or a failure message. A login to an account the profile already holds ends in success too, and a success the runtime cannot publish is published as a failure that says why, so a client that waits for the end always receives it. Clients apply that patch directly because its one-shot progress and settlement are not part of a later snapshot. Replacing a login or changing the default auth profile first publishes failure for the displaced operation. Clients correlate only the operation they initiated; unrelated refreshes and sibling accounts carry no matching settlement. Settlement metadata does not authorize subscription spend and never becomes consent truth. API-key login remains synchronous and does not use OAuth settlement correlation.
+
+External-login questions derive from each project's provider-owned account conditions, not from notification history or session Ask User state. Every scoped auth view carries the same optional project-wide `loginQuestions` list, including questions for background sessions on another profile. Descriptors carry an opaque question ID, provider and account identities, and display names for the provider, account, and captured profile. No auth directory, config directory, or credential crosses this boundary. Clients serialize question presentation through their existing modal or confirmation owner; presentation queues hold no answer authority.
+
+Automatic Yes sends `auth.login` with `loginQuestionId`; the runtime atomically claims that exact asking episode before starting the existing login. A duplicate or stale Yes returns `ignored`, not success. The runtime resolves the captured owned profile and account rather than the current foreground or default profile. No or question close sends `auth.login.decline` for the episode. Owner-driven removal closes the client's question without another mutation. Automatic sign-in dismissal sends `auth.login.cancel` scoped to its question and, when available, its operation ID, including dismissal before the initial URL arrives. Stale answers, cancellation, and late completion cannot affect another episode or operation. Manual login replacement retains its existing behavior.
+
+Successful same-account automatic login renews waiters only after the existing login and catalog settlement succeed. Failure, cancellation, or a different returned account declines the original episode without rebinding requests or accounting identity. Automatic operation settlement publishes to all active or observed auth scopes, so the originating client can remain on another profile. Questions and waiters remain project-local; no cross-project answer authority exists.
+
+### 7. Protocol Failures And Connection State Are Typed
+
+Runtime failures cross the boundary through the smallest typed discriminant clients currently consume, separating Flywheel invariant violations from internal runtime failures without making every RPC invent its own taxonomy.
+
+The public event read boundary and the authenticated private executor intake accept additive protocol evolution. A receiver skips an unsupported event type, cursor scope or domain, or patch kind without ending its connection. Known frames remain strictly validated, and current writers validate every frame before publication to either transport. Skipping a patch never advances a cursor: an ensuing gap in a supported cursor recovers through its existing authoritative snapshot path.
+
+The runtime advertises support for client diagnostics on the connected frame. A supporting client reports an unsupported event through a typed best-effort RPC, once per event signature and session during a connection. The runtime writes only the descriptor and session identity to its diagnostic log, not the event payload or durable fact store. Diagnostic delivery never blocks rendering or user actions. An older runtime receives no unsupported diagnostic RPC.
+
+Runtime health is a first-class client signal, and recovery is not. The shared connection reattaches on its own after a drop, ends the lane with a `reconnect_required` invalidation, opens the next lane so every receiver reads its view again, and stays healthy while it does; no surface renders a reconnecting state, because a drop that heals is not information a person can act on. The connection reports a fault once the runtime has not answered for its fault window, counted from its last frame, and an attach sent after that window also fails or goes unanswered for a heartbeat interval, or at once when the attach failed with a cause the runtime itself reported, and every surface renders that one fault with the runtime's words and the remedy for its lane, until the runtime answers again and the fault clears itself. A host whose presence shows the runtime appear, such as a page that sees its project listed open after its machine restarted, ends a fault without the runtime's words, because that fault rested on attaches to the absent runtime; a new fault window starts and the next attach goes out at once. A best-effort read whose deadline passes while the lane hears no frame failed with the connection's silence, not on its own (`explainedByConnection`), so no surface reports it, and a background reader reads it again. Startup failure before the socket exists reaches the same fault through the client that started the runtime. A runtime that answers states what a person should know about the process that runs its sessions on the `executor` workspace domain: a field in the workspace snapshot and a latest-collapsible patch at each change. It reads `serving`, `restarting` with how that process last stopped, or `recovery_failed` with why its sessions could not be restored when the runtime knows a cause. The account service states what a person should know about itself on the `service` workspace domain, the same way: a field in the workspace snapshot and a latest-collapsible patch at each change, from the account's one value, which every open project serves. It reads `serving`, or `crash_loop` with the latest cause while the start that answers follows more than one stop within a minute of a listen and has not yet served its own first minute (ADR-007). The shared client core derives the one runtime health every surface renders from those two states and the connection's own health, ranked by what the person can act on: a connection fault first, then `recovery_failed`, which asks the person to act, then `crash_loop`, then `restarting`, because the service serves the executor's project. A surface renders `restarting` and `crash_loop` each as one warning that names the cause and asks nothing, and `recovery_failed` as a fault with the runtime's words and the remedy for its lane, the way it renders a lost runtime; only a lost runtime offers a reconnect.
+
+The live event lane guarantees its own liveness. One versioned protocol contract owns the heartbeat interval and silence deadline used by every serving and client placement. The server merges that heartbeat into every subscription, and the client converts silence past the shared bounded deadline into a failed subscription. That deadline is the lane's only clock: the client's RPC transport (`protocol/client/socket-protocol.ts`) carries no request-level ping of its own, because a second, stricter deadline that a phone painting a turn cannot meet would fail a healthy lane and reconnect it from scratch. The deadline is twelve heartbeats, because the heartbeat rides the same ordered lane as the data and a burst queued ahead of it on a slow link must not read as death.
+
+The lane's backpressure is end to end, through a byte window. The client acknowledges each chunk of a stream once, in chunk order, after the chunk enters its bounded stream buffer. The runtime counts each acknowledgement as credit for the encoded bytes of the oldest unacknowledged chunk of that stream. It sends a stream's batches at once while less than a fixed byte window (`STREAM_WINDOW_BYTES`) for each stream on each connection is unacknowledged, and it holds that stream only while a full window is. A link that keeps up never fills the window, so a frame waits for no round trip, and frames that arrive during a hold wait in the runtime's outbound queue. The hold starts after the write returns, so it holds no writer lock, and replies to queries on the same connection go out during it.
+
+Two copies of Effect's socket protocol keep this contract, and each names the other: the runtime's server protocol (`orchestration/app-service/runtime-protocol-socket-server.ts`), which owns the window, and the client's transport (`protocol/client/socket-protocol.ts`), which sends the acknowledgements.
+
+A client that falls behind keeps its lane in the other direction too: the runtime's per-connection outbound queue is sliding, dropping the oldest frames past its bounded capacity rather than the connection. Capacity accommodates the measured initial catalog and budget burst with headroom. After draining a batch that overflowed, the stream appends one global overflow invalidation behind the retained frames. The recovery signal stays outside the sliding queue so further overflow cannot discard it. Each affected client resnapshots even when a dropped cursor has no later patch to reveal the gap. Repeated drops before a drain share one invalidation, without a reconnect or a polling timer.
+
+Every relay socket follows the heartbeat rule with its own interval and deadlines in the relay contract: the runtime's control socket, every device socket, and the runtime's side of every data channel ping the relay, and the relay answers without waking the runtime's object. A client closes a socket and dials again once its pings go unanswered for the deadline, counted in ticks of its own timer so that a stalled event loop or a suspended page adds no silence, and a data channel that ends this way ends its lane at once, because a link that died without a close never reports one. A busy socket is never silent: a ping waits behind the bytes queued ahead of it, so a tick at which the socket still passes its backlog to the network counts nothing, the relay counts every frame a socket sends as well as its pings, and the sealed channel sends a write as bounded frames so the far end hears from a long transfer throughout it. The relay judges each socket by the heartbeat the socket states when it dials, or by the presence deadline when it states none: it drops a silent socket from presence, and closes a silent data channel together with its peer, so a device behind a machine whose link died learns it within the channel's deadline rather than the event lane's minute. The relay watches for silence only while a device is connected, because only then can a dead socket keep a person waiting, and it keeps what each socket stands for, a device's waiting `open` and a control socket's pairing windows, in that socket's own attachment: a storage write is a moment in which a platform storage fault resets the relay's object and ends every socket of the machine at once, so the relay writes only the device allowlist when it changes and the next sweep while a device is connected. The edge can also drop the machine's side of a channel while the relay hears no close; the machine sees that end at once, as a connection that ended with no close handshake or a heartbeat that gave up, and reports the channel ended on its control socket, again on each attach until the channel's deadline has passed, and the relay closes the device's side at once, or opens a device's channel again under a new id when it had not yet accepted the dead one, so no write goes into a dead lane for longer than the device takes to dial again. A device that dials again meanwhile waits on its placement, and the relay sends its `open` to whichever control socket serves the placement next. The account-service handshake carries the protocol version, and every editor connection authenticates and selects its project before it opens the runtime protocol. Without guaranteed traffic, a wedged event stream is observationally identical to an idle one; with it, silence is evidence. Recovery is the ordinary invalidation, resnapshot, and resubscribe path, not a parallel mechanism.
+
+Startup is visible before the protocol serves. Anything that blocks the load path must reach the user as a notice with progress, and the blocking window is exactly the time before the RPC server exists, so startup status cannot ride the event lane. The account service reports process startup as typed records on its stdout handshake before the socket exists. Concurrent callers join that start and receive its latest phase and subsequent reports. Each accepted socket then answers account attach with `starting`, named phase records, `failed`, or `ready` before runtime protocol framing begins. The state Worker supplies workspace preparation and migration progress through its control port. The canonical project opening retains its latest phase for joining clients and clears its subscribers on settlement. Every phase is one step of the ordered startup catalog in the published protocol (`src/protocol/startup-steps.ts`): a producer names the step, and the catalog alone gives it its label and its place among all steps of a start. Inside one build, producers pass only the step id, across the state Worker's port as well, which always runs the build that spawned it and refuses a step its catalog does not hold. The account service turns a step into its label and count once, as it writes the phase frame to the socket, so no producer can write words of its own. A step that a start does not run, such as the account service start when the service already serves or the stored-fact upgrade when nothing was stored since its passes last ran, keeps its place, so the count moves forward to the next step that runs and the last step fills it. The stored-fact upgrade is one step; each of its passes names itself and its own count inside that step's label ("Upgrading stored facts, pass 2 of 24: memory facts") and leaves the count at the step, so the count always counts steps of the whole start. Phase frames carry only that label and that count, in the shape every reader of the protocol decodes, and each socket sends them before `ready`. A client that started the service reads process records from its stdout spool, and every client reads its own project readiness from the socket it will use. The terminal shows each phase as its label over one progress bar of the whole start while its first chat is pending, with faults taking precedence. A process or project failure therefore reaches the load screen instead of leaving it silent, and the first lane's opening supersedes startup state after attach. Startup status never enters the runtime event lane; the wire vocabulary after readiness stays the connected event, ordered patches, and heartbeats.
+
+---
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- Snapshots, queries, and live patches form one explicit client contract, packaged so a new out-of-tree client compiles against `src/protocol/` alone.
+- The local socket, authenticated loopback WebSocket, and relay reverse transport serve one protocol, so the web client and the TUI cannot drift in call or recovery semantics, and the web client's hosts cannot drift from each other because they mount one bundle.
+- Shared classification keeps every receiver on one definition of patch order, and the packaged intake keeps out-of-tree clients on one recovery implementation.
+- A dropped or reordered patch is detected instead of silently corrupting a pane.
+- New live domains must declare both recovery and intake.
+- User mutations cannot expire under a client-local read deadline.
+- Project notification clients recover from the authoritative workspace snapshot and fold raised, resolved, and dismissed patches in cursor order.
+- Durable and live transcript output share one coordinate and bounded recovery path.
+
+### Negative
+
+- Every receiver must retain cursor and held-patch state.
+- Recovery still pays the cost of folding authoritative history.
+- The deliberately small error taxonomy loses detail callers do not yet consume.
+- Runtime invalidation requires an authoritative read rather than trusting local projections: a lane break defers that read to the next lane, and every other reason reads at once.
+
+### Risks
+
+- A publisher bypasses the sequencer and invents ordering metadata.
+- A client imports a runtime module instead of the protocol package, or restates intake classification locally.
+- A new domain is added without a real receiver or suitable recovery policy.
+- A mutation is misclassified as best-effort and becomes ambiguous under timeout.
+- A second transcript offset, snapshot stitcher, or replay cache recreates duplicate authority.
+
+---
+
+## Rejected Alternatives
+
+### Unsequenced Event Delivery
+
+Rejected because loss and reordering become silent stale-state bugs.
+
+### One Independent Position Space Per Publisher
+
+Rejected because clients cannot reason uniformly about continuity and publishers become ordering authorities.
+
+### Separate Durable And Live Transcript Coordinates
+
+Rejected because every boundary movement requires offset translation, seeding, and restitching.
+
+### Full Transcript Snapshot Recovery
+
+Rejected because a local intake fault would require work proportional to session lifetime.
+
+### In-Memory Replay Ring
+
+Rejected while bounded authoritative reads already recover cursor gaps without another retention authority.
+
+### Client Deadlines On Mutations
+
+Rejected because timeout cannot distinguish “not delivered” from “delivered but response delayed.”
+
+---
+
+## Governing Rules
+
+- A client reaches runtime state only through `RuntimeClient`. An out-of-tree client compiles against `src/protocol/` and nothing else in `src`; the in-tree TUI compiles against `orchestration/`.
+- The local socket, authenticated loopback WebSocket, and relay reverse transport are equal transports of one protocol; a transport never changes call, cursor, or recovery semantics, and a transport that crosses an untrusted relay seals its frames below the protocol.
+- Publishers never mint `position` or `prevPosition`; the sequencer is the sole ordering authority.
+- Every domain declares delivery policy, patch content, and client intake.
+- A module reaches a client only through the slot map: protocol row selectors, a strip and surfaces per client, a presence hook in the terminal, and a toolbar badge on the web, registered in that client's module registry. No client host names a module.
+- Receivers handle apply, stale, gap, hold, replay, and resync through one ordered path, using the packaged `protocol/client/` intake and delta core rather than restating classification.
+- The web client in `webview/` is the one out-of-tree client. Every host supplies its required payload of a `Socket` layer, a capability object, and a theme; the client branches on capability and container width, never on host identity; and a host never reconciles session or transcript state of its own.
+- Every client states its protocol version at every event attach, and a web client also states its bundle identity. The runtime refuses an identity that is not its own with `client-mismatch`, and the client answers by loading itself again from the runtime, restoring the reader's place it saved before the reload. The runtime then refuses another protocol version, or none, with `protocol-mismatch`, which names the release and protocol version the runtime serves. The attach's fields keep their meaning in every version, and a runtime ignores attach fields it does not know. Both checks live in the protocol's event attach and nowhere in a transport.
+- A terminal answers a service of the current build that speaks another protocol by re-executing into the build that serves, carrying its place in one versioned launch-state file. A terminal attached through the relay installs the release the machine runs and re-executes into it, or waits while the machine runs an earlier release; it never changes the machine's service. A person's `/restart` re-executes it the same way into its lane's entry once its account service has been replaced. A launch state it cannot read starts it plain; it never fails a start.
+- Every unary call declares `Lossless` or `BestEffort`; mutations are lossless.
+- Session Changes Viewed mutations carry the exact latest file-edit source identity. Clients never derive review validity from a list revision or content hash.
+- `auth.login`, `auth.login.answer`, `auth.login.complete`, `auth.login.decline`, `auth.login.cancel`, `auth.account.disconnect`, and `auth.account.rename` are lossless, and they are the only way any client changes credentials. The client host opens reported URLs, except for a client that states the person is at the runtime's machine, for which the runtime opens them. Browser completion targets the exact active operation, and every continuing OAuth flow reports its progress and settles exactly once on the `auth` cursor with correlated success or failure. Automatic recovery claims an opaque question once, targets its captured profile, and scopes cancellation to its own question and operation. Replacing a manual login or its default profile settles the displaced operation first.
+- Durable history and the runtime tail share one absolute transcript coordinate.
+- The account holds the executor-resolved chat-default projection; `chat_default.changed` carries its complete latest-collapsible replacement, while explicit work captures its own profile and provider admission.
+- The account's generations owner holds the executor state; `executor.changed` carries its complete latest-collapsible replacement, and clients derive runtime health from it in the shared client core, never beside it.
+- The account service holds its one service state; `service.changed` carries its complete latest-collapsible replacement to every open project, and clients derive runtime health from it with the executor state in the shared client core.
+- Broad union schemas enumerate direct leaf members, and public event plus private executor intake skip additive unknown variants without weakening validation of known frames.
+- Skill invocation crosses as a lossless mutation carrying the package id; the runtime, not the client, renders the package body.
+- Built-in commands cross as `command.invoke` carrying the descriptor id and the rendered catalog revision; the runtime executes the handler and answers a closed outcome, and no client executes a module handler or names a command id. A descriptor that names the surface it `opens` needs no invocation.
+- Auth status snapshots and `usage.open`, `usage.accounts`, `usage.spend`, `usage.history`, and `usage.refresh` use defaults or session scope; profile mutations declare defaults, session, or defaults-and-session. Keep exact-window account quota reads, explicit-range ledger spend reads, and independently ranged provider-observation history distinct.
+- Keep auth and provider-model views secret-free. Address provider-model state only through the discriminated singleton target or subscription `{ providerId, accountCorrelationId }` target, and carry profile identity and eligibility revision in stale-sensitive mutations. Do not expose `instanceId` or `servingCandidate` in provider-model payloads. Live login questions carry the account identity required by reconnect, with an opaque question ID as answer authority and no filesystem path.
+- Keep subscription-spend consent in the auth view secret-free; writes echo opaque envelope IDs, profile ID, authority incarnation and revision, and the client auth-cursor position. Treat the cursor as review context and leave consent compare-and-set semantics to ADR-020.
+- Keep project notification actions closed, keep targeted auth actions opaque and account-specific, deliver notification gaps through durable resnapshot, treat dismiss as a lossless mutation of one exact source episode, and discard the complete notification aggregate rather than block startup when strict replay fails.
+- Declare a module's queries and mutations in its protocol home beside its domains; the registry composes their unions, codecs, policies, and coverage, and the runtime routes each by discriminant with no module case.
+- Do not add a second output index, offset, full-history recovery path, or replay cache without a measured requirement and new ADR.
+- Deliver runtime config snapshots and mutations through this protocol without redefining ADR-018's config semantics.
