+# ADR-020: Subscription Spend Consent
+
+**Kind**: Implementation decision
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Deciders**: Flywheel Core Team
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+Authentication for an account with included quota authorizes that quota only. Flywheel authorizes its paid rollover capacity only when fresh provider evidence establishes that capacity and the profile-local durable consent authority records an accepted decision for the exact paid-use envelope. Prepaid and automatic billing are independent envelopes and default off. Provider evidence never becomes consent, local request accounting never becomes consent, and local usage ceilings are policy rather than consent.
+
+Direct API and aggregator credentials authorize their paid-use paths by their presence and never enter this authority. An external account that its engine explicitly classifies as usage-only also bypasses this authority: its one `direct_api` candidate uses normal metered billing and retains subscription account identity. Claude's `enterprise_usage_based` seat classification establishes this exception; a plan name, billing platform, or missing allowance windows does not. Such accounts derive no rollover envelopes, paid-use questions, or detection failures, and existing consent decisions remain stored. Included subscription quota, direct API billing, aggregator billing, and a profile-local ceiling for a pooled subscription seat bypass the subscription consent gate. A ceiling can hold included capacity below the provider allowance; it cannot enable prepaid or automatic spend. ADR-017's request ledger accounts for every served mode without changing this exact-account consent boundary.
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. Exact Paid-Use Envelopes
+
+One envelope identifies the resolved auth profile, provider, stable credential instance, versioned one-way account-subject digest, and either `prepaid` or `automatic` mode. The profile separates authorization for the same account used through different profiles. The stable instance and subject digest prevent one account from inheriting another account's decision. The mode keeps prepaid funds independent from automatic billing.
+
+A token refresh that preserves the profile, provider, stable instance, and account subject preserves envelope identity. Replacing the profile, provider, stable instance, account subject, or mode creates a different envelope with no decision. The authority stores no credential values, tokens, or raw account subjects.
+
+Canonical spend classes and candidate order remain under ADR-005. Auth-profile placement and stable account identity remain under ADR-013. Provider-reported prepaid and automatic-billing observations remain under ADR-017.
+
+### 2. Durable Profile-Local Decisions
+
+Each resolved profile directory contains one SQLite consent authority with an immutable incarnation and monotonic revision. It stores only `accepted` or `declined` decisions keyed by canonical envelope identity. Absence is `unreviewed`. The transient protocol projection groups envelopes by verified account, publishes profile identity, usage generation, authority incarnation and revision, and orders accounts and their `prepaid` then `automatic` modes canonically. Provider display and billing data appear once per account. Each mode carries its exact evidence, decision, envelope id, and whether positive evidence permits enabling it.
+
+Both mode envelopes exist for every verified account with included quota. Missing mode evidence is the mode's explicit `unknown` value, so a prior decision remains visible and revocable without becoming eligible for paid admission. A question is asked only for positive evidence with an unreviewed decision. Closing Provider Settings or failing to verify an account writes nothing. Closing the paid-use question is an answer: it records `declined` for that account's modes that were waiting for one and leaves earlier decisions alone. For one account, a save from Provider Settings reviews modes that are currently enableable or previously accepted; unavailable unreviewed modes remain untouched. A save binds the complete canonical profile envelope set for stale safety but writes decisions only for its explicit `reviewedEnvelopeIds`, and `acceptedEnvelopeIds` is a subset of that reviewed set. Hidden sibling-account decisions and absences remain unchanged.
+
+The authority incarnation distinguishes a replacement authority from an older file with the same revision. One profile writer lock excludes concurrent profile mutations, and one atomic publication commits decision changes with the next revision. The durable authority and revision, not the lock, own the result.
+
+### 3. Stale-Safe Review And Mutation
+
+A consent mutation binds the resolved profile, usage generation, authority incarnation, authority revision, and exact canonical ordered envelope set shown to the client. Before writing, the runtime derives that set again from both rollover modes of every verified account with included quota and rejects any usage-generation change before evaluating acceptance against evidence. Under the profile writer lock, the store strictly reloads current credential identity and revalidates each provider, stable instance, and subject digest before one atomic publication. The runtime validates that reviewed envelopes belong to the canonical set and that accepted envelopes are a subset. The store updates only reviewed envelopes. Any changed usage generation, authority, envelope set, account identity, or lineage rejects the mutation without a partial decision. An accepted envelope whose current evidence is unavailable can remain accepted or be declined, but it cannot authorize paid use; a client cannot newly accept an unavailable envelope. Decisions never carry across profile, provider, stable-instance, or subject lineage.
+
+The auth delivery cursor position identifies the client context in which review occurred. It is not the compare-and-set authority for consent. ADR-010 owns the secret-free auth view, mutation transport, cursor delivery, stale result vocabulary, and OAuth settlement correlation. OAuth settlement does not authorize paid use.
+
+Only the active resolved profile is reviewed. Changing profiles exposes that profile's independent authority. A replacement profile, account subject, stable instance, or envelope mode receives no grandfathered decision from the prior identity.
+
+### 4. Immutable Request Authorization And Admission
+
+Each provider request performs admission by joining fresh, current-generation provider usage evidence to durable decisions without copying evidence into the consent store. The resulting request capture is immutable and binds each accepted authorization to the exact envelope, resolved profile, usage generation, authority incarnation, and authority revision. It separately captures profile policy such as a pooled-seat ceiling. Policy can remove an otherwise available included candidate, but only evidence plus accepted consent can admit prepaid or automatic use. One request-local capture governs the complete fixed candidate walk or stream; later evidence, consent, or policy changes affect the next request.
+
+The runtime memoizes this immutable join only while both inputs are exact matches: the SHA-256 digest of the complete consent-authority bytes and the identity of the published usage snapshot. Every provider request rechecks the authority digest and the Usage service's live freshness verdict for every supported account before using the memoized capture. The exact publication is a cache input only while every supported account still exposes current spend sources through that service; unsupported, stale, and failed siblings impose no freshness condition. Concurrent cold captures coalesce only when their independently read initial digest and eligible usage snapshot identity are exact matches. Expired supported evidence prevents reuse, joining, and insertion even if no usage publication or authority bytes changed. A changed authority, a new usage publication, a local mutation, or a profile lifecycle change also forces the existing locked capture path. That path remains the sole authorizer and preserves healthy sibling accounts on a cache miss. The cache has no independent freshness timer or authorization filter. The memoized value is a derived optimization; the profile-local authority remains the sole consent truth.
+
+Admission fails closed. A `prepaid` or `automatic` subscription candidate is eligible only when the captured provider evidence supports that exact mode and the captured consent decision is accepted for that exact envelope. Missing, stale, failed, unsupported, unknown, unavailable, empty, or identity-inconsistent evidence cannot authorize paid use. A declined or unreviewed envelope skips only that exact mode before provider construction and does not create retry, suppression, or planner state.
+
+Static provider contribution and construction remain under ADR-015. Provider evidence and freshness remain under ADR-017. Evidence establishes capability only; it never grants permission.
+
+### 5. The Paid-Use Question And Notifications
+
+The paid-use question is one dedicated view for one exact account. It names the provider and the signed-in identity, states in plain words that the provider reports paid capacity for the account and that Flywheel keeps paid use off until the person allows it, shows one checkbox per paid mode with its evidence, and offers Continue. Continue records the ticked modes as `accepted` and the rest of the account's reviewable modes as `declined`. Escape, the frame's close affordance, and `q` record `declined` for the modes that were waiting for an answer and close the question. No key is swallowed, no path exits Flywheel, and a write that fails still closes the question with a visible notice, because an unanswered mode is already off and the question returns at the next boundary. An unanswered mode never blocks any other work.
+
+Subscription login settlement refreshes the profile's usage evidence and consent view before it completes, so the connecting client decides from evidence rather than racing it. When the settled account has a mode waiting for an answer, the TUI ends the connection flow on that account's paid-use question and returns to the provider list after the answer; the connected account is asked first, and an earlier account of the same provider that still waits is asked otherwise. If settlement cannot read the evidence, no question appears, the client says paid use stays off, and later detection is handled below.
+
+At startup and profile activation, the TUI asks the question once for the first account of the active profile that waits for an answer, at a safe foreground boundary and never during an active model turn. Because closing records an answer, the question does not return for that account. Two waiting accounts take two answers, one question each, the next asked from the committed consent view. Editor and phone clients never interrupt work: they keep a pending indicator and their Provider Settings view, explain beside each unanswered mode that it is off until ticked and saved, and deep-link an explicit login continuation or notification to the exact account and mode.
+
+If paid capacity becomes detectable later, the current request remains blocked and the runtime publishes a durable project notification through ADR-019. The notification action identifies the exact provider, account correlation, and first unreviewed positive envelope; it opens the paid-use question while that account still waits for an answer and Provider Settings afterwards. Presentation history is not consent authority. Paid admission remains blocked until the exact envelope has an accepted durable decision. Detection failure does not block included quota and does not create a decision.
+
+Provider Settings shows provider health and quota status for every exact account. For an account with included quota it also shows, under one `Paid use in Flywheel` heading, the provider-reported balance, accrued use, or cap evidence for prepaid and automatic billing with one checkbox-only control per mode, and the provider billing destination when available. The account's signed-in identity, or the masked tail of its API key, is content beside its status, plan, and health, never part of the view's title. A mode with unavailable evidence and no accepted decision is passive and cannot be selected. A previously accepted mode remains visible and revocable when evidence becomes unavailable, but unavailable evidence never authorizes paid use. The surface edits only the selected account while every save binds the complete canonical profile envelope set. Opening provider billing keeps Provider Settings open, never opens model selection, and mutates neither provider billing nor Flywheel consent. Model selection is a separate explicit action. A normal account edit can close without saving. Provider billing controls remain the spend-limit authority; Flywheel records permission only. Its profile-local pooled-seat ceiling is independent routing policy, not a consent decision or a universal monetary limit.
+
+## Authority Boundaries
+
+Spend consent is kernel behavior because its evidence is an input to provider admission. Its view travels through the `auth` protocol domain, and its modal and sheet are provider-authentication surfaces. It cannot be disabled independently of safe admission. The optional `subscription-usage` module owns inspection, polling, and threshold notices; turning it off preserves consent review, explicit auth refresh, provider availability, affordability, and request settlement.
+
+- ADR-005 owns canonical spend classes and candidate ordering. Every provider failure can advance to an already-authorized later spend class; fallback never grants subscription paid-use consent.
+- ADR-010 owns auth cursor views, secret-free protocol types, lossless consent mutations, mutation results, and OAuth settlement delivery.
+- ADR-013 owns profile directory placement, immutable profile identity, stable credential-instance identity, and account-subject lineage.
+- ADR-015 owns static provider composition, request planning, lazy provider construction, and the pre-effect candidate walk that consumes captured authorization.
+- ADR-017 owns provider-reported usage observations, spend-source normalization, freshness, usage generations, and evidence availability.
+- ADR-019 owns durable project-notification episodes, delivery, dismissal, resolution, and the exact Auth account-and-envelope action.
+- ADR-020 owns subscription paid-use envelope identity, durable decisions, mutation revalidation, review completeness, immutable authorization capture, and paid subscription admission.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Subscription login cannot silently authorize a charge after included quota is exhausted. Each profile, account identity, and paid mode receives an explicit durable decision, while token rotation preserves a valid decision when account identity does not change. Evidence and consent remain separate authorities, and request admission can prove both inputs without a live store read during candidate construction.
+
+Detection failure and stale review fail closed. A newly detected paid mode can require review after login or while a project is open, so durable notification is necessary even though foreground review occurs only at safe boundaries.
+
+## Rejected Alternatives
+
+### Authentication As Paid-Use Consent
+
+Rejected because subscription authentication is required for included quota and does not prove permission for prepaid depletion or automatic billing. Direct API and aggregator credentials are already explicit paid credentials and do not need a second consent authority.
+
+### Provider Evidence As Consent
+
+Rejected because funded balance or provider enablement describes capability, not user authorization. Storing evidence beside decisions would create parallel state and could preserve authorization after evidence or identity changed.
+
+### Profile-Wide Billing Toggle
+
+Rejected because it would collapse distinct providers, accounts, and prepaid and automatic modes into one unsafe grant.
+
+### Cursor Position As Consent Revision
+
+Rejected because delivery order describes client context, while the profile-local incarnation, revision, envelope set, and current account identity define whether a write is still valid.
+
+### Usage Accounting
+
+Flywheel keeps profile-scoped request accounting under ADR-017, but that ledger does not grant consent, control provider billing, or claim invoice authority. Flywheel has no universal spend limit, billing toggle, or purchase path. A profile-local ceiling may hold a pooled subscription seat below its provider-reported allowance, but it is routing policy and never authorizes paid capacity. Provider usage evidence establishes capacity, exact-account decisions establish consent, the request ledger estimates served usage, and the provider invoice remains authoritative for charges.
+
+## Governing Rules
+
+- Treat authentication for an account with included quota as authorization for that quota only.
+- Keep prepaid and automatic billing as independent, default-off envelopes.
+- Key each envelope by profile, provider, stable instance, versioned subject digest, and mode. Store no credentials, tokens, or raw subjects.
+- Preserve envelope identity across token refresh only when profile and account identity remain unchanged.
+- Store accepted and declined decisions only. Derive unreviewed state from decision absence, and derive review need from positive current evidence plus an unreviewed decision.
+- Derive both mode envelopes for every verified account with included quota in canonical account order and `prepaid`, then `automatic` mode order. Preserve missing evidence as explicit `unknown` so accepted or declined decisions remain visible and revocable but cannot enable paid admission.
+- Write nothing when Provider Settings closes or account verification fails. Closing the paid-use question records `declined` for that account's modes that were waiting for an answer. A Provider Settings save reviews every currently enableable or previously accepted mode and leaves unavailable unreviewed modes untouched.
+- Bind every mutation to profile, authority incarnation and revision, and the exact canonical envelope set. Require accepted ids to be a subset of reviewed ids, update only reviewed envelopes, revalidate current account identity under the profile writer lock before atomic publication, and never carry a decision across identity lineage.
+- Treat delivery cursor position as review context, never as consent compare-and-set authority.
+- Review only the active profile and never grandfather a decision across profile, provider, stable-instance, subject, or mode replacement.
+- Capture accepted authorization immutably with current provider evidence at every provider request admission, and keep that capture fixed through its candidate walk.
+- Reuse a request capture only while the exact consent-authority digest and exact usage publication remain unchanged and every supported account still passes the Usage service's live freshness check; coalesce concurrent cold captures only when both initial inputs match exactly and the publication remains eligible for reuse.
+- Require both eligible provider evidence and accepted exact-envelope consent for prepaid or automatic subscription admission.
+- Bypass this authority for included quota, direct API credentials, aggregator credentials, and explicitly classified usage-only external accounts.
+- Do not interrupt active turns for review. Publish later detection through durable project notifications and keep paid admission blocked until decision.
+- Ask the paid-use question for an exact account at the end of its connection flow, and once at a safe startup or profile-activation boundary for the first active-profile account still waiting. The question explains itself, is always closable, records an answer when closed, never swallows input, never exits Flywheel, and asks the next waiting account from the committed view.
+- Do not add a universal or root spend limit, billing toggle, or purchase path. Keep profile-scoped request accounting under ADR-017, provider invoices as charge authority, and any pooled-seat ceiling profile-local and separate from consent.
+- Keep Provider Settings uniform for every exact account: show health and quota status, use checkbox-only paid-mode controls, keep unavailable-unaccepted modes passive, keep accepted-unavailable modes revocable, and make billing a non-mutating action that never opens model selection.
